{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To solve for the unique odd integer \\( t \\) such that \\( 0 < t < 23 \\) and \\( t+2 \\) is the inverse of \\( t \\) modulo 23, we need to find \\( t \\) such that:\n\n\\[ t(t+2) \\equiv 1 \\pmod{23} \\]\n\nThis can be rewritten as:\n\n\\[ t^2 + 2t \\equiv 1 \\pmod{23} \\]\n\nRearranging the terms, we get:\n\n\\[ t^2 + 2t - 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{23} \\]\n\nWe need to find the roots of the quadratic congruence \\( t^2 + 2t - 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{23} \\). To do this, we can use the quadratic formula \\( t = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\) where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 2 \\), and \\( c = -1 \\). Plugging in these values, we get:\n\n\\[ t = \\frac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{2^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-1)}}{2 \\cdot 1} = \\frac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{4 + 4}}{2} = \\frac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{8}}{2} = \\frac{-2 \\pm 2\\sqrt{2}}{2} = -1 \\pm \\sqrt{2} \\]\n\nWe need to find the value of \\( \\sqrt{2} \\) modulo 23. To do this, we need to find an integer \\( x \\) such that \\( x^2 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{23} \\). We can test values to find \\( x \\):\n\n","prefix_end":948,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":false,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,11625,369,279,4911,10322,7546,17767,259,1124,8,1741,429,17767,220,15,366,259,366,220,17,18,1124,8,323,17767,259,10,17,1124,8,374,279,27949,315,17767,259,1124,8,59864,220,17,18,11,582,1184,311,1477,17767,259,1124,8,1741,429,1447,78045,259,1155,10,17,8,1124,57317,220,16,1124,79,2593,90,17,18,92,1124,2533,1986,646,387,58524,438,1447,78045,259,61,17,488,220,17,83,1124,57317,220,16,1124,79,2593,90,17,18,92,1124,2533,49,682,81,8595,279,3793,11,582,633,1447,78045,259,61,17,488,220,17,83,481,220,16,1124,57317,220,15,1124,79,2593,90,17,18,92,1124,2533,1654,1184,311,1477,279,19703,315,279,79151,30169,84,763,17767,259,61,17,488,220,17,83,481,220,16,1124,57317,220,15,1124,79,2593,90,17,18,92,1124,568,2014,653,419,11,582,646,990,279,79151,14806,17767,259