91,198],"messages":[{"content":"Solve the problem step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nFind the unique $\\textbf{odd}$ integer $t$ such that $0<t<23$ and $t+2$ is the inverse of $t$ modulo $23$.","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"506c4226de923eeddb8dabb323acd9920dfc308da473fe7b8c1bca0f1275e35c"},"seconds":18.715787887573242,"seed":766374190688681676,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve for the unique odd integer \\( t \\) such that \\( 0 < t < 23 \\) and \\( t+2 \\) is the inverse of \\( t \\) modulo 23, we need to find \\( t \\) such that:\n\n\\[ t(t+2) \\equiv 1 \\pmod{23} \\]\n\nThis can be rewritten as:\n\n\\[ t^2 + 2t \\equiv 1 \\pmod{23} \\]\n\nRearranging the terms, we get:\n\n\\[ t^2 + 2t - 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{23} \\]\n\nWe need to find the roots of the quadratic congruence \\( t^2 + 2t - 1 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{23} \\). To do this, we can use the quadratic formula \\( t = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\) where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = 2 \\), and \\( c = -1 \\). Plugging in these values, we get:\n\n\\[ t = \\frac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{2^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot (-1)}}{2 \\cdot 1} = \\frac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{4 + 4}}{2} = \\frac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{8}}{2} = \\frac{-2 \\pm 2\\sqrt{2}}{2} = -1 \\pm \\sqrt{2} \\]\n\nWe need to find the value of \\( \\sqrt{2} \\) modulo 23. To do this, we need to find an integer \\( x \\) such that \\( x^2 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{23} \\). We can test values to find \\( x \\):\n\n\\[ 1^2 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{23} \\]\n\\[ 2^2 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{23} \\]\n\\[ 3^2 \\equiv 9 \\pmod{23} \\]\n\\[ 4^2 \\equiv 16 \\pmod{23} \\]\n\\[ 5^2 \\equiv 25 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{23} \\]\n\nSo, \\( \\sqrt{2} \\equiv 5 \\pmod{23} \\) or \\( \\sqrt{2} \\equiv -5 \\equiv 18 \\pmod{23} \\). Therefore, the solutions to the quadratic congruence are:\n\n\\[ t = -1 + 5 = 4 \\]\n\\[ t = -1 - 5 = -6 \\equiv 17 \\pmod{23} \\]\n\nSince we are looking for the unique odd solution, we check \\( t = 4 \\) and \\( t = 17 \\). Clearly, \\( t = 4 \\) is even, so the only odd solution is \\( t = 17 \\).\n\nTo verify, we check if \\( t = 17 \\) satisfies the original condition:\n\n\\[ t+2 = 19 \\]\n\\[ 17 \\cdot 19 = 323 \\]\n\\[ 323 \\div 23 = 14 \\text{ remainder } 1 \\]\n\\[ 323 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{23} \\]\n\nThus, the unique odd integer \\( t \\) is \\( \\boxed{17} \\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{17}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"number_theory:29"}
